{"content_hash": "268689a709ed1e73", "version_id": 272, "article_id": 28, "level_code": "en_a2", "lang": "en", "level": 2, "level_label": "A2 初级", "title": "Injury Study: Half of Patients Died or Disabled", "title_zh": "受伤者研究：一半病人死亡或残疾", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-global-injury-crisis-highlights-urgent.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49492358448_70ce40b0d5_b.jpg", "credit": "NavyMedicine · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 228, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "A new study looked at almost 9,000 injured people in Africa and South Asia. It found that many were dead or disabled after three months.", "lead_zh": "一项新研究调查了非洲和南亚近9000名受伤者。研究发现，很多人在三个月后死亡或残疾。", "preview": "Researchers studied 8,858 injury patients in 19 hospitals. The hospitals were in Ghana, Pakistan, Rwanda and South Africa. After three month", "preview_zh": "研究人员调查了19家医院的8858名受伤病人。这些医院位于加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非。三个月后，47.6%的病人已经死亡或残疾。6.1%在医院死亡。9.7%在三个月内死亡。40.6%有中度或重度残疾。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 262, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@cec1515/content/audio/28/en_a2.mp3", "duration": 109.825, "size_bytes": 662688, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 271, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@cec1515/content/data/versions/272.json", "paragraphs": ["Researchers studied 8,858 injury patients in 19 hospitals. The hospitals were in Ghana, Pakistan, Rwanda and South Africa. After three months, 47.6% of patients had died or had a disability. 6.1% died in the hospital. 9.7% died within three months. 40.6% had a moderate or severe disability.", "The study found that how patients travel to hospital matters. Patients who came by ambulance had worse results. They had 86% higher odds of death or disability. They also had more than double the odds of death within three months. But the researchers say ambulances are not the problem.", "Professor Justine Davies said, 'Investment in ambulance systems alone is unlikely to improve outcomes.' She said services must work together and have enough resources. Dr. Leila Ghalichi said countries should not just copy rich-country models. They should build trauma care based on local needs.", "The study also found that faster treatment did not always help. Patients who had some delays were less likely to be disabled later. Age was linked to worse outcomes. But gender, wealth and education had little effect in the hospital. After leaving hospital, richer and better-educated patients recovered better.", "Injuries cause about 4.4 million deaths every year. 90% of these deaths are in low- and middle-income countries. Road crashes caused 50% of the injuries in this study. The middle age of patients was 31 years. 76.8% were male."], "paragraphs_zh": ["研究人员调查了19家医院的8858名受伤病人。这些医院位于加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非。三个月后，47.6%的病人已经死亡或残疾。6.1%在医院死亡。9.7%在三个月内死亡。40.6%有中度或重度残疾。", "研究发现，病人如何去医院很重要。坐救护车去医院的病人结果更差。他们死亡或残疾的几率高86%。他们三个月内死亡的几率也高出一倍多。但研究人员说，问题不在救护车本身。", "贾斯汀·戴维斯教授说：“只投资救护车系统，不太可能改善结果。”她说，服务必须互相配合，并且有足够的资源。莱拉·加利奇博士说，国家不应只照搬富裕国家的模式。他们应该根据本地需求建立创伤护理。", "研究还发现，更快的治疗并不总是有帮助。有一些延迟的病人，后来残疾的可能性反而更低。年龄与更差的结果有关。但性别、财富和教育在医院里影响不大。出院后，更富有和受教育更好的病人恢复得更好。", "每年，受伤导致约440万人死亡。其中90%的死亡发生在低收入和中等收入国家。在这项研究中，车祸造成了50%的受伤。病人的中位年龄是31岁。76.8%是男性。"], "body": "Researchers studied 8,858 injury patients in 19 hospitals. The hospitals were in Ghana, Pakistan, Rwanda and South Africa. After three months, 47.6% of patients had died or had a disability. 6.1% died in the hospital. 9.7% died within three months. 40.6% had a moderate or severe disability.\n\nThe study found that how patients travel to hospital matters. Patients who came by ambulance had worse results. They had 86% higher odds of death or disability. They also had more than double the odds of death within three months. But the researchers say ambulances are not the problem.\n\nProfessor Justine Davies said, 'Investment in ambulance systems alone is unlikely to improve outcomes.' She said services must work together and have enough resources. Dr. Leila Ghalichi said countries should not just copy rich-country models. They should build trauma care based on local needs.\n\nThe study also found that faster treatment did not always help. Patients who had some delays were less likely to be disabled later. Age was linked to worse outcomes. But gender, wealth and education had little effect in the hospital. After leaving hospital, richer and better-educated patients recovered better.\n\nInjuries cause about 4.4 million deaths every year. 90% of these deaths are in low- and middle-income countries. Road crashes caused 50% of the injuries in this study. The middle age of patients was 31 years. 76.8% were male.", "body_zh": "研究人员调查了19家医院的8858名受伤病人。这些医院位于加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非。三个月后，47.6%的病人已经死亡或残疾。6.1%在医院死亡。9.7%在三个月内死亡。40.6%有中度或重度残疾。\n\n研究发现，病人如何去医院很重要。坐救护车去医院的病人结果更差。他们死亡或残疾的几率高86%。他们三个月内死亡的几率也高出一倍多。但研究人员说，问题不在救护车本身。\n\n贾斯汀·戴维斯教授说：“只投资救护车系统，不太可能改善结果。”她说，服务必须互相配合，并且有足够的资源。莱拉·加利奇博士说，国家不应只照搬富裕国家的模式。他们应该根据本地需求建立创伤护理。\n\n研究还发现，更快的治疗并不总是有帮助。有一些延迟的病人，后来残疾的可能性反而更低。年龄与更差的结果有关。但性别、财富和教育在医院里影响不大。出院后，更富有和受教育更好的病人恢复得更好。\n\n每年，受伤导致约440万人死亡。其中90%的死亡发生在低收入和中等收入国家。在这项研究中，车祸造成了50%的受伤。病人的中位年龄是31岁。76.8%是男性。", "text": "Researchers studied 8,858 injury patients in 19 hospitals. The hospitals were in Ghana, Pakistan, Rwanda and South Africa. After three months, 47.6% of patients had died or had a disability. 6.1% died in the hospital. 9.7% died within three months. 40.6% had a moderate or severe disability. The study found that how patients travel to hospital matters. Patients who came by ambulance had worse results. They had 86% higher odds of death or disability. They also had more than double the odds of death within three months. But the researchers say ambulances are not the problem. Professor Justine Davies said, 'Investment in ambulance systems alone is unlikely to improve outcomes.' She said services must work together and have enough resources. Dr. Leila Ghalichi said countries should not just copy rich-country models. They should build trauma care based on local needs. The study also found that faster treatment did not always help. Patients who had some delays were less likely to be disabled later. Age was linked to worse outcomes. But gender, wealth and education had little effect in the hospital. After leaving hospital, richer and better-educated patients recovered better. Injuries cause about 4.4 million deaths every year. 90% of these deaths are in low- and middle-income countries. Road crashes caused 50% of the injuries in this study. The middle age of patients was 31 years. 76.8% were male.", "vocab": [{"word": "injury", "pos": "noun", "zh": "受伤", "note": "指身体受到的伤害"}, {"word": "patient", "pos": "noun", "zh": "病人", "note": "接受治疗的人"}, {"word": "hospital", "pos": "noun", "zh": "医院", "note": "看病的地方"}, {"word": "disability", "pos": "noun", "zh": "残疾", "note": "身体或精神上的长期障碍"}, {"word": "ambulance", "pos": "noun", "zh": "救护车", "note": "运送病人的车"}, {"word": "death", "pos": "noun", "zh": "死亡", "note": "生命的结束"}, {"word": "study", "pos": "noun", "zh": "研究", "note": "为了了解某件事而做的调查"}, {"word": "outcome", "pos": "noun", "zh": "结果", "note": "事情最后的情况"}], "title_original": "Global injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care", "summary_original": "A study of almost 9,000 injury patients across Africa and South Asia has found that nearly half were dead or disabled after three months—highlighting the need for smarter, locally tailored trauma-care systems.", "body_original": "Global injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care\nby\nUniversity of Birmingham\nedited by\nSadie Harley\n, reviewed by\nAndrew Zinin\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\npeer-reviewed publication\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nA study of almost 9,000 injury patients across Africa and South Asia has found that nearly half were dead or disabled after three months—highlighting the need for smarter, locally tailored trauma-care systems.\nPublishing its\nfindings in\nThe Lancet Global Health\n, the international research group led by University of Birmingham experts reveals that the biggest influences on survival and recovery are not just the injuries themselves, but how people move through the health care system.\nInjuries cause an estimated 4.4 million deaths annually worldwide, with 90% occurring in LMICs. This is the first large multicountry study to examine mortality and disability outcomes among hospitalized injury patients across low- or middle-income countries (LMIC).\nThe study reveals that access to appropriate care, the route patients take to the hospital and the type of services available all play an important role in these outcomes.\nAnalyzing data from 8,858 patients treated in 19 urban and rural hospitals across Ghana, Pakistan, Rwanda and South Africa, the Global Health Group on Equitable Access to Quality Health Care for Injured People in Four Low- or Middle-Income Countries (Equi-injury) reveals an enormous and often overlooked global health burden:\n47.6% of patients either died or were left with moderate-to-severe disability within three months of hospital discharge.\n6.1% died in the hospital, 9.7% had died within three months, and 40.6% were living with moderate-to-severe disability three months after discharge.\nPatients arriving at the hospital by ambulance had significantly worse outcomes, with 86% higher odds of death or disability and more than double the odds of mortality within three months compared with those using other forms of transportation.\nLead author Professor Justine Davies, from the University of Birmingham, said, \"One of our most surprising findings was that measures often assumed to improve outcomes—such as ambulance transport and rapid transfer to major hospitals, which are similar to highly centralized trauma services—were not consistently linked with better recovery or survival.\n\"This does not mean that ambulances cause worse outcomes but suggests that investment in ambulance systems alone is unlikely to improve outcomes unless services are coordinated and appropriately resourced.\"\nThe findings challenge assumptions that health care models developed in wealthier countries can simply be copied elsewhere. Instead, the study argues that countries should develop trauma and injury-care systems based on local evidence, resources and patient needs.\nCorresponding author Dr. Leila Ghalichi, from the University of Birmingham, said, \"This does not mean ambulances or specialist trauma centers are ineffective. Rather, it suggests that simply investing in expensive emergency systems is not enough.\n\"LMICs should be cautious about investing heavily in ambulance services and specialist, centralized trauma centers without ensuring they are well coordinated, properly staffed and supported by strong health systems. Better data and further research are needed to understand which trauma-care investments improve patient outcomes.\"\nThe study notes that stopping first at a nearby hospital for emergency stabilization may sometimes be beneficial, especially if a specialist hospital is far away. If a major trauma hospital is close by, going directly there may improve outcomes.\nResearchers found no evidence that reaching a hospital more quickly reduced the risk of death. Patients who experienced some delays were less likely to be disabled later—challenging the common belief that faster treatment always leads to better outcomes after injury.\nAge was linked to worse outcomes, but gender, wealth and education had little effect on survival while patients were in the hospital. However, once out of the hospital, wealthier and better-educated patients tended to recover better, suggesting that access to rehabilitation and follow-up care may play an important role.\nSome 90% of global injury deaths occur in LMICs and are estimated to account for around 10% of the global burden of disease. Yet trauma care receives far less attention than infectious diseases or chronic conditions in global health policy.\nCountry-level differences were striking. Patients in South Africa had the lowest odds of disability but the highest odds of mortality, while patients in Rwanda experienced the lowest odds of mortality. Meanwhile, patients in Pakistan experienced substantially higher odds of the combined outcome of death or disability than those in Ghana.\nThe study population reflects the demographics of injury worldwide. The median age was 31 years, 76.8% were male, and road traffic collisions accounted for 50% of all injuries recorded. Orthopedic injuries were the most common injury type, affecting 39.2% of patients.\nPublication details\nJustine Davies et al, Mortality and disability after hospitalisation for injuries in low-income and middle-income countries: a cohort study,\nThe Lancet Global Health\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1016/j.langlo.2026.104053\nJournal information:\nThe Lancet Global Health\nKey medical concepts\nInjury\nMortality\nClinical categories\nEmergency medicine\nHospital medicine\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nProvided by\nUniversity of Birmingham\nWho's behind this story?\nSadie Harley\nBSc Life Sciences & Ecology. Microbiology lab background with pharmaceutical news experience in oil, gas, and renewable industries.\nFull profile →\nAndrew Zinin\nMaster's in physics with research experience. Long-time science news enthusiast. Plays key role in Science X's editorial success.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nGlobal injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-global-injury-crisis-highlights-urgent.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T12:35:25.451601+00:00", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Researchers", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.787, "sm": 100, "em": 787, "cs": 0, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "studied", "s": 0.8, "e": 1.2, "sm": 800, "em": 1200, "cs": 12, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "8,858", "s": 1.212, "e": 2.962, "sm": 1212, "em": 2962, "cs": 20, "ce": 25, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "injury", "s": 2.975, "e": 3.375, "sm": 2975, "em": 3375, "cs": 26, "ce": 32, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "patients", "s": 3.388, "e": 3.888, "sm": 3387, "em": 3887, "cs": 33, "ce": 41, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "in", "s": 3.9, "e": 4.1, "sm": 3900, "em": 4100, "cs": 42, "ce": 44, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "19", "s": 4.112, "e": 4.55, "sm": 4112, "em": 4550, "cs": 45, "ce": 47, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "hospitals", "s": 4.562, "e": 5.325, "sm": 4562, "em": 5325, "cs": 48, "ce": 57, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": ".", "s": 5.325, "e": 6.2, "sm": 5325, "em": 6200, "cs": 57, "ce": 58, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "The", "s": 6.2, "e": 6.35, "sm": 6200, "em": 6350, "cs": 59, "ce": 62, "si": 1}, {"i": 10, "w": "hospitals", "s": 6.362, "e": 6.975, "sm": 6362, "em": 6975, "cs": 63, "ce": 72, "si": 1}, {"i": 11, "w": "were", "s": 6.987, "e": 7.1, "sm": 6987, "em": 7100, "cs": 73, "ce": 77, "si": 1}, {"i": 12, "w": "in", "s": 7.112, "e": 7.212, "sm": 7112, "em": 7212, "cs": 78, "ce": 80, "si": 1}, {"i": 13, "w": "Ghana", "s": 7.225, "e": 7.637, "sm": 7225, "em": 7637, "cs": 81, "ce": 86, "si": 1}, {"i": 14, "w": ",", "s": 7.637, "e": 7.812, "sm": 7637, "em": 7812, "cs": 86, "ce": 87, "si": 1}, {"i": 15, "w": "Pakistan", "s": 7.812, "e": 8.488, "sm": 7812, "em": 8487, "cs": 88, "ce": 96, "si": 1}, {"i": 16, "w": ",", "s": 8.488, "e": 8.675, "sm": 8487, "em": 8675, "cs": 96, "ce": 97, "